# scry's default rule pack (S4)
#
# The canonical text is mirrored by `Scry.demoPackText` in
# `lib/Scry/RuleExamples.wiz`, which the `scry-taint` tests parse. The format is
# implemented by `Scry.parseRulePack` (`lib/Scry/Rules.wiz`):
#
#   rule ID
#     kind taint | forbidden
#     severity error | warning | note
#     message TEXT
#     source NAME result | arg K
#     sanitise NAME result | arg K
#     sink NAME arg K | args        (args: every argument, for a variadic callee)
#     call NAME                     (forbidden rules)
#   end
#
# Names match with `*` as a wildcard over the whole callee name.
#
# External models (`lib/Scry/Models.wiz`) add the effects of libc/syscall calls
# after these lines: a model source is the same as a `source` line, a model sink
# for a `(call, argument)` the pack does not claim is added with the model's rule
# id, and a model `copy`/`copyArgs` becomes a memory store. Sources that arrive at
# a program entry rather than at a call (`main`'s argv) are seeded by the model's
# entry-source hook.

rule command-injection
  kind taint
  severity error
  message Attacker-controlled data reaches a command interpreter
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  source getline arg 0
  source fread arg 0
  source getopt result
  source getopt_long result
  sanitise escape_shell result
  sanitise shellescape result
  sanitise sanitize arg 0
  sink system arg 0
  sink popen arg 0
  # execl(path, arg0, …, NULL): every word is part of the command line, including the
  # path, so any argument is a sink (`args`). execle's last argument is envp, so only
  # the path is claimed; the v-forms take the path and the argv vector.
  sink execl args
  sink execlp args
  sink execle arg 0
  sink execv arg 0
  sink execv arg 1
  sink execvp arg 0
  sink execvp arg 1
  sink execve arg 0
  sink execve arg 1
  sink execvpe arg 0
  sink execvpe arg 1
  # posix_spawn(pid, path, file_actions, attrp, argv, envp): the program path and the
  # argv vector carry the command; envp is not a command word.
  sink posix_spawn arg 1
  sink posix_spawn arg 4
  sink posix_spawnp arg 1
  sink posix_spawnp arg 4
end

rule format-string
  kind taint
  severity warning
  message Attacker-controlled data is used as a format string
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  source getline arg 0
  source fread arg 0
  sanitise escape_shell result
  sanitise sanitize_format result
  sink printf arg 0
  sink fprintf arg 1
  sink sprintf arg 1
  sink snprintf arg 2
  sink vprintf arg 0
  sink vfprintf arg 1
  sink vsprintf arg 1
  sink vsnprintf arg 2
  sink syslog arg 1
  sink vsyslog arg 1
  sink dprintf arg 1
  sink vdprintf arg 1
  sink asprintf arg 1
  sink vasprintf arg 1
  sink easprintf arg 1
end

rule gets-overflow
  kind forbidden
  severity error
  message gets cannot bound its write; use fgets with a size
  call gets
end

# A hint, not a finding. `strcpy`/`strcat` are unbounded writes, but whether the
# destination is a fixed-size array (a real overflow) or a buffer whose capacity
# this analysis has not established (for example a `char *` from a caller) is a
# bounds question the taint domain does not answer. The rule therefore reports a
# `note`; it is not evidence of a CVE on its own. `strlcpy`/`strncat` are bounded
# and are not called here. `snprintf` truncation is deliberately NOT a sanitiser:
# a truncated command can still be dangerous, and truncation is not a content
# sanitiser.
rule strcpy-overflow
  kind forbidden
  severity note
  message hint, not a finding: unbounded strcpy/strcat; destination capacity unknown
  call strcpy
  call strcat
end
